const RC_PASSAGE =
  "Passage: Urban trees do more than decorate a street. A single mature tree can absorb significant amounts of carbon dioxide each year, reduce nearby air temperatures by several degrees during summer, and lower stormwater runoff by intercepting rainfall in its canopy. Cities that invest in tree cover often see lower electricity use for cooling, because shaded buildings need less air conditioning. Yet urban trees face constant stress from compacted soil, pollution, and limited root space, so many planted saplings do not survive their first decade. Long-term tree survival, researchers argue, depends less on how many trees are planted and more on how well they are maintained afterward.\n\n";

const RC_PASSAGE_2 =
  "Passage: The spread of remote work has quietly reshaped how cities grow. For decades, transport planners assumed that most people needed to live within a practical commute of a city centre, which kept housing demand concentrated and prices high near business districts. Once a meaningful share of employees no longer commuted daily, that assumption weakened. Some workers moved farther from city centres in search of larger homes at lower prices, trading a short commute for one taken only a few times a month. This shift has not been uniform: roles requiring in-person collaboration, hands-on equipment, or direct customer contact saw little change, while knowledge-based roles saw the most movement. Economists remain divided on the long-run effect. Optimists argue the shift will ease pressure on overcrowded cities and revive smaller towns. Skeptics counter that it mainly benefits workers who were already well paid, while leaving low-wage, location-bound workers to absorb rising local costs without any of the new flexibility.\n\n";

const RC_PASSAGE_4 =
  "Passage: For most of the twentieth century, nutrition science treated calories as largely interchangeable: a calorie from sugar and a calorie from protein were assumed to affect the body in essentially the same way, provided the total count matched. Over the past two decades, this assumption has come under sustained challenge. Researchers have shown that different macronutrients trigger distinct hormonal responses, influence hunger and satiety differently, and are processed through different metabolic pathways. A diet high in refined carbohydrates, for instance, can provoke sharper insulin spikes than a calorically equivalent diet richer in fibre and protein, with downstream effects on fat storage and appetite. None of this means calories are irrelevant — energy balance still matters for weight change — but it complicates the older, simpler model in which only the total mattered. Public health messaging has been slow to catch up with this nuance, partly because 'count your calories' is a far easier instruction to communicate than a description of hormonal and metabolic variation. Critics of the newer research caution against overcorrecting: some popular diet trends have exaggerated preliminary findings into sweeping claims that are not yet well supported by long-term evidence. The more defensible position, most nutrition scientists now argue, lies between the two extremes — calories matter, but so does their source, and reducing either side of that equation to a slogan risks misleading the public.\n\n";

const RC_PASSAGE_3 =
  "Passage: Honeybees communicate the location of food sources through a behaviour known as the waggle dance. A foraging bee that finds a rich source of nectar returns to the hive and moves in a figure-eight pattern, waggling its body along the straight middle section of the pattern. The angle of this straight run relative to the sun indicates the direction of the food, while the duration of the waggling indicates distance — a longer waggle means a farther source. Other bees sense these movements through vibration and touch, in near-total darkness, and can then fly directly to a source several kilometres away without having seen it themselves. This system is remarkable not just for its accuracy but because it is entirely symbolic: the dance does not resemble the journey itself, yet it reliably transmits both direction and distance. Researchers studying the waggle dance have drawn comparisons to human language, noting that both rely on arbitrary signals to represent real-world information not present at the moment of communication.\n\n";

